    {
        "name": "Gastric Dilation (Bloat)",
        "name_ja": "胃拡張（鼓脹症）",
        "symptoms": {"abdominal_distension", "abdominal_pain", "lethargy", "appetite_loss", "teeth_grinding", "rapid_breathing"},
        "description": "Gastric Dilation (Bloat) in rabbits is an acute, life-threatening emergency characterized by rapid accumulation of gas and/or fluid in the stomach with gastric distension. CRITICAL: This condition progresses rapidly to shock, cardiovascular collapse, and death if untreated (can be fatal within 2-6 hours of symptom onset). Rabbits have no mechanism to vomit; accumulated gas cannot be expelled, creating a closed system with rapidly escalating gastric pressure.",
        "description_ja": "ウサギの胃拡張（鼓脹症）は胃への急速なガス/液体蓄積と胃拡張が特徴の急性、命に関わる緊急事態。重大：未治療でショック・心血管虚脱・死亡に急速に進行（症状発症2-6時間で致死的）。ウサギは嘔吐機構がなく、蓄積ガスは排出不可で、閉鎖系で急速に胃圧上昇。",
        "urgency": "emergency",
        "recommended_tests": ["abdominal_xray", "physical_exam", "blood_gas_analysis", "blood_work", "blood_pressure", "ecg"],
        "causes": "Gastric dilation in rabbits is multifactorial: (1) DIETARY TRIGGERS—sudden feed composition changes (pellet/hay type/ratio sudden shift), low-fiber diet (<15% crude fiber predisposes bloat, commercial pellets often 8-12%), excessive fruit/carbohydrates (>5% diet = fermentable substrate promoting bacterial gas production), moldy/spoiled feed (triggers dysbacteriosis, bacterial gas-producing species proliferate), non-food items (plastic, bedding ingestion). (2) GASTRIC MOTILITY DYSFUNCTION—stress (any stressor: transport, loud noise, predator presence, handling, social conflict triggers inhibitory sympathetic input, suppresses gastric contractions), pain (tooth disease, prior GI disease, systemic illness causes reflexive gastric atony), dehydration (reduced mucosal secretion, decreased gastric fluid consistency promotes gas pocketing), medications (opioids cause gastric dysmotility; avoid butorphanol/buprenorphine in rabbits unless carefully titrated), prolonged anorexia prior to bloat onset (gastric flora dysbiosis developing, when feeding resumes, dysbiotic bacteria produce copious gas). (3) INFECTION/DYSBIOSIS—bacterial proliferation (Clostridium, Bacteroides dysbiosis from prior antibiotic exposure or sudden diet change; dysbiotic gram-negative bacteria produce lipopolysaccharide triggering ileus), parasitic load (heavy coccidia/nematode burden impairs motility). (4) GASTRIC OBSTRUCTION—hairballs (felting of ingested fur, especially during shedding season; not pathognomonic for bloat but can coexist), strictures (post-inflammatory), foreign material impaction (plastic, bedding). (5) SYSTEMIC ILLNESS—sepsis (peritonitis from perforated GI tract, severe respiratory infection), shock states (hypoxia, hypotension), CNS disorder (encephalitis, toxemia). (6) BREED/INDIVIDUAL PREDISPOSITION—some rabbits show recurrent bloat tendency despite diet management (possible genetic variation in gastric motility).",
        "causes_ja": "ウサギの胃拡張は多因子性：(1) 食事トリガー—給食組成急変（ペレット/干し草タイプ/比率急変）、低繊維食（粗繊維<15%で鼓脹素因、商業ペレット多くは8-12%）、過剰果物/炭水化物（食事の>5% = 発酵性基質が細菌ガス産生促進）、カビ汚染飼料（dysbacteriosis誘発）、非食品物（プラスチック、敷料摂取）。(2) 胃運動障害—ストレス（輸送、大音声、捕食者存在、取扱い、社会的葛藤が抑制交感神経入力、胃収縮抑制）、疼痛（歯疾患、先行GI疾患、全身疾患が反射的胃無気力）、脱水（粘膜分泌減少、胃液粘稠度低下がガスポケッティング促進）、薬物（オピオイドが胃運動異常）、長期摂食不足。(3) 感染/Dysbiosis—細菌増殖、寄生虫感染。(4) 胃閉塞—毛玉、狭窄、異物。(5) 全身疾患—敗血症、ショック状態。(6) 品種/個体素因—再発鼓脹傾向。",
        "pathophysiology": "Gastric dilation pathophysiology in rabbits: (1) PRIMARY PHASE (0-30 min)—gastric fermentation or fluid/gas accumulation rapidly expands stomach volume. Stomach distension activates vagal mechanoreceptors triggering reflexive gastric atony (vagal inhibition of antral contractions). Accumulating gas/fluid prevents normal peristalsis. (2) PRESSURE ESCALATION PHASE (30 min-2 hr)—gastric volume increases exponentially (normal rabbit stomach capacity ~40 mL; distended stomach can contain 200-400 mL). Increased intragastric pressure (>15 cmH₂O) compresses gastric vessels, reducing mucosal perfusion. Ischemia develops within 45-90 min, initiating mucosal injury and bacterial translocation. Pressure also compresses splanchnic veins, impeding portal circulation. (3) CARDIOVASCULAR COLLAPSE PHASE (1-6 hrs)—gastric distension increases intra-abdominal pressure, compressing caudal vena cava (CVA) → reduced venous return → decreased cardiac preload → hypotension and shock. Systemic inflammatory response triggered by bacterial lipopolysaccharide (LPS) from translocated gram-negative bacteria intensifies vasodilation, worsening hypovolemic/septic shock. Metabolic acidosis develops (lactate accumulation from splanchnic hypoxia). (4) ORGAN FAILURE PHASE (2-6+ hrs)—acute gastric rupture possible if pressure continues unchecked (risk increases significantly >180-200 mmHg intragastric pressure); rupture rapidly causes peritonitis and death. Myocardial depression from circulating cytokines (TNF-α, IL-1) and direct septic effects reduce contractility. Respiratory compromise from elevated abdominal pressure splinting diaphragm. Multi-organ dysfunction (acute kidney injury from shock, hepatic dysfunction from portal stasis) develops. Mortality approaches 100% if untreated beyond 6 hours.",
        "pathophysiology_ja": "ウサギの胃拡張病態生理：(1) 初期相（0-30分）—胃発酵またはガス/液体蓄積が急速に胃容量拡張。胃拡張が迷走神経機械受容体活性化し反射的胃無気力（迷走神経が前庭収縮抑制）誘発。蓄積ガス/液体が正常蠕動防止。(2) 圧力上昇相（30分-2時間）—胃容量が指数関数的増加（正常ウサギ胃容量~40 mL；拡張胃200-400 mL含有可）。胃内圧上昇（>15 cmH₂O）が胃血管を圧迫し粘膜灌流低下。45-90分で虚血発展、粘膜損傷と細菌転座開始。圧力が門脈循環を障害する脾臓静脈も圧迫。(3) 心血管虚脱相（1-6時間）—胃拡張が腹腔内圧上昇、後大静脈を圧迫→静脈帰流減少→心臓前負荷低下→低血圧とショック。全身炎症応答が転座グラム陰性菌からのLPSで誘発され血管拡張悪化、容量欠乏性/敗血症ショック。代謝性アシドーシス発展。(4) 臓器不全相（2-6時間以上）—急性胃破裂が可能で圧力が継続未確認の場合リスク有意に増加；破裂は急速に腹膜炎と死亡。心筋抑制はサイトカイン（TNF-α、IL-1）と直接敗血症効果から発展。呼吸障害は腹腔内圧上昇が横隔膜を分割。多臓器機能障害発展。未治療で6時間超えると死亡率100%に接近。",
        "treatment": "IMMEDIATE EMERGENCY TREATMENT: (1) DIAGNOSTIC CONFIRMATION & SEVERITY ASSESSMENT—abdominal radiographs (lateral + ventrodorsal views) to confirm gastric distension (>5-6 cm diameter = significant distension), assess for gastric rupture (free air peritoneal cavity = surgical emergency), evaluate for secondary torsion (stomach position abnormal). Blood work: PCV/hemoglobin (assess hemorrhage/hypovolemia), electrolytes (hyperkalemia from muscle ischemia, hyponatremia from third-spacing), lactate (elevated = splanchnic hypoxia severity marker), blood glucose (hypoglycemia common in shock), liver enzymes (assess hepatic compromise). Abdominal ultrasound if available (assess gastric wall thickness, detect peritoneal effusion suggesting rupture). Blood pressure monitoring (systolic <90 mmHg = shock; guide fluid resuscitation). (2) SHOCK MANAGEMENT & FLUID RESUSCITATION—IV crystalloid fluid bolus: LRS 60-100 mL/kg rapid IV (20 mL/kg over 15 min, reassess, repeat if shock persists). Target: MAP ≥65 mmHg (preferably >75 mmHg). Placement of 20-22 gauge catheter in ear or hindleg vein. Continue maintenance fluids 50-75 mL/kg/day IV after initial stabilization. Monitoring: urine output (>0.5 mL/kg/hr = adequate renal perfusion), mucous membrane color (should progress from pale/tacky to pink/moist within 15-20 min of fluid resuscitation). Colloid consideration if not responding adequately to crystalloid (hetastarch 10-15 mL/kg IV slowly). (3) GASTRIC DECOMPRESSION—orogastric tube placement under anesthesia (gentle technique essential; severe gastric torsion risk if aggressive manipulation): pass 14-16 Fr nasogastric/orogastric tube slowly with jaw held open by assistant. Placement success indicated by passage of tube into stomach without resistance; aspirate gas/fluid via syringe immediately. Minimize manipulation—aggressive decompression rarely necessary if tube placed; gravity drainage sufficient. ALTERNATIVE: trocar decompression (percutaneous needle or catheter inserted directly into gastric lumen) if orogastric tube placement unsuccessful or if severe gastric tension/rupture imminent—technique risky, reserved for critical cases. Successful decompression should relieve abdominal pain within minutes; vital signs should begin stabilizing. (4) PAIN MANAGEMENT—critical for shock management (pain worsens catecholamine release, increases heart rate, peripheral vasoconstriction; pain control improves overall hemodynamics). Meloxicam 0.3-0.5 mg/kg IV/SC (preferred NSAID; faster onset than PO). Opioid analgesics should be AVOIDED or used with extreme caution (cause further gastric dysmotility; if absolutely necessary for severe pain, use very low-dose buprenorphine 0.02-0.03 mg/kg IM once, monitored closely). (5) ANTIBIOTIC THERAPY—mandatory (bacterial translocation occurring; sepsis risk very high). Enrofloxacin 10-15 mg/kg IV/SC q12h (broad-spectrum, good splanchnic penetration) PLUS metronidazole 15-20 mg/kg IV q12h (anaerobic coverage; Clostridium, Bacteroides commonly involved). Continue 7-14 days. NOT penicillins/cephalosporins (risk severe GI dysbiosis). (6) PROKINETIC AGENTS—AFTER initial decompression. Cisapride 0.5 mg/kg PO q8h (preferred prokinetic in rabbits; enhances gastric contractions) starting once rabbit can tolerate oral intake (typically 2-4 hours post-decompression). Metoclopramide 0.4-0.5 mg/kg IV/SC/PO q6-8h (secondary prokinetic, inferior to cisapride but has antiemetic effect if nausea present). Continue 7-14 days. (7) GASTROPROTECTION—sucralfate 100-500 mg PO q6-8h (forms mucosal protectant barrier, important for post-ischemic mucosal healing) × 10-14 days. Omeprazole 1-2 mg/kg PO q24h (proton pump inhibitor, reduces acid in event of ulceration risk) × 5-7 days. Famotidine 0.25-0.5 mg/kg IV/SC/PO q12h (H₂ antagonist) alternative if omeprazole unavailable. (8) DIETARY MANAGEMENT POST-ACUTE PHASE—DO NOT feed for 6-8 hours post-decompression to allow gastric rest. Then: introduce small frequent meals of timothy hay (high fiber, promotes normal motility) + small amount of leafy greens (romaine, kale) every 2-3 hours, hand-fed. Avoid: pellets (high density, risk re-distension), fruit/carbohydrates (fermentation risk), fresh hay is safest choice. Gradually increase hay amount over 48-72 hours; reintroduce pellets only if normal appetite and defecation confirmed for 24-48 hours. Syringe-feed high-fiber hay slurry if appetite suppressed (timothy hay + water blended, 10-30 mL q2-3h). (9) ABDOMINAL MASSAGE & MOVEMENT—gentle abdominal massage (light circular motions, 2-3 min q2-3h) stimulates myenteric plexus, promotes motility recovery. Encourage gentle movement/exercise (allow controlled hopping) after stabilization, not forced (anxiety worsens motility). (10) SURGICAL INTERVENTION—reserved for: (a) gastric rupture (peritonitis, free air on radiographs), (b) persistent gastric distension despite aggressive decompression + medical therapy × 4-6 hours, (c) identification of mechanical obstruction (torsion, foreign body, stricture). Surgical exploration includes careful visualization of stomach (assess viability), decompression via gastrotomy if needed (closure with 4-0/5-0 absorbable sutures), removal of obstructing foreign body, careful assessment for ischemic necrosis (if present, full-thickness resection with anastomosis required—high mortality, ~40-50% post-op survival). Prognosis for surgical cases much poorer than medical decompression alone (mortality 30-50% surgical vs. 5-20% medical management if caught early). (11) MONITORING POST-TREATMENT—continuous assessment for recurrence: monitor abdominal pain response (pain should improve within 1 hour decompression), vital signs (heart rate should decrease as shock resolves), appetite recovery (eating within 2-4 hours is good sign), fecal output (normal cecotrophy should resume within 6-12 hours). Monitor for complication development: peritonitis signs (fever, increasing pain, severe lethargy despite pain management), dehydration (reassess q4h), re-distension risk (repeat abdominal palpation/radiographs if pain recurs). Hospitalization typically requires 24-48 hours of continuous IV fluids + monitoring.",
        "treatment_ja": "直ちに緊急治療：(1) 診断確認と重症度評価—腹部X線（側面+背腹像）胃拡張確認（>5-6 cm径 = 有意拡張）、胃破裂評価（腹腔遊離ガス = 外科緊急）、二次捻転評価。血液検査：PCV/ヘモグロビン、電解質（筋虚血からの高カリウム血症、第三間隙からの低ナトリウム血症）、乳酸（上昇 = 脾臓虚血重症度マーカー）、血糖（ショックで低血糖一般的）、肝酵素。腹部超音波利用可能なら。血圧監視（収縮期<90 mmHg = ショック）。(2) ショック管理と輸液蘇生—IV結晶質液ボーラス：LRS 60-100 mL/kg迅速IV。目標：MAP ≥65 mmHg。20-22ゲージカテーテル耳または後肢静脈に配置。初期安定化後保守液50-75 mL/kg/日IV続行。監視：尿産生（>0.5 mL/kg/時間 = 腎灌流適切）、粘膜色。膠質検討。(3) 胃除圧—経口胃チューブ配置麻酔下（温和技術重大）：14-16 Fr鼻胃チューブをゆっくり通過。成功は抵抗なくチューブが胃に通過することで示唆；シリンジで直ちにガス/液体抽吸。最小限操作。トロカー除圧代替え（経皮針またはカテーテル胃腔に直接挿入）経口胃チューブ配置失敗時または重症胃緊張/破裂差迫時—技術危険。成功除圧は数分で腹痛緩和すべき。(4) 疼痛管理—重大。メロキシカム0.3-0.5 mg/kg IV/SC。オピオイド鎮痛薬は避けるべき。(5) 抗菌薬療法—強制。エンロフロキサシン10-15 mg/kg IV/SC q12h プラス メトロニダゾール15-20 mg/kg IV q12h。7-14日。(6) 運動促進薬—初期除圧後。シサプリド0.5 mg/kg PO q8h。メトクロプラミド。(7) 胃保護—スクラルフェート100-500 mg PO q6-8h。オメプラゾール1-2 mg/kg PO q24h。(8) 食事管理—除圧後6-8時間摂食なし。その後：小量頻回給餌。(9) 腹部マッサージと運動。(10) 外科的介入—予約済み。(11) 治療後監視。",
        "prevention": "CRITICAL PREVENTION of gastric bloat recurrence (70-80% of rabbits that experience one episode are at high risk for recurrence): (1) DIETARY MANAGEMENT—HIGH-FIBER DIET MANDATORY: minimum 18-20% crude fiber (aim 20-30% for optimal GI health); timothy-hay based diet is gold standard (timothy hay 70-80% of total diet, commercial timothy hay pellets 15-20%, small amount green vegetables 5%). Verify pellet crude fiber content at purchase (quality brands specify >15% crude fiber; discount brands often 8-12%). High-quality pellets: timothy-based > alfalfa-based (alfalfa higher calcium, more palatable but less fiber density). Store pellets in cool/dry location; avoid pellets >6 months old (nutritional degradation). Do NOT feed: commercial treat pellets (colored balls, yogurt bits—excessive sugars, fermentable), fruit in quantity (limit <2-3% total diet), vegetables high in oxalates without moderation (spinach, chard—bind calcium), moldy/wet hay (bacterial overgrowth). (2) AVOID SUDDEN FEED CHANGES: if diet switch necessary, introduce new hay/pellet gradually over 7-10 days (gradual replacement, 10-15% new diet per day, allowing cecal flora adaptation). NEVER abrupt pelleted diet switch in stressed rabbit (highest bloat risk window). (3) STRESS REDUCTION—stress is primary trigger for post-bloat recurrence; minimize: excessive handling (especially during adjustment period post-discharge), loud noises (vacuum, television, predator presence), overcrowding (minimum 2-3 m² living space per rabbit), social conflict (pair-housed rabbits require compatible temperaments). Environmental enrichment critical (varied hay types, willow branches, hiding areas, toys). (4) DENTAL DISEASE PREVENTION—regular dental checks (every 3-6 months); malocclusion or tooth disease → pain → gastric atony → bloat risk. Early veterinary intervention for any chewing difficulty. (5) MAINTAIN HYDRATION: provide fresh water ad lib (water bottle or bowl, verified working daily); dehydration significantly increases bloat risk (gastroprotective fluid viscosity decreases). (6) WEIGHT MANAGEMENT: obesity (rabbits >20% above ideal body weight) increases bloat risk; regular exercise and appropriate feeding prevent excess weight. (7) MEDICATIONS PRECAUTIONS: avoid opioids (cause gastric dysmotility); if necessary for other conditions, monitor closely for bloat signs. Avoid prolonged antibiotic courses (dysbiosis risk)—only use antibiotics when specifically indicated, with concurrent probiotic supplementation if possible (Saccharomyces or Bacillus species considered safe in rabbits; avoid Lactobacillus which may increase gas production in some rabbits). (8) EXERCISE & MOVEMENT: 2-4 hours daily free-range hopping/movement (promotes normal gastric motility, prevents ileus, provides environmental enrichment). Even gentle movement routine prevents post-operative ileus better than cage rest. (9) AGE/RISK STRATIFICATION: young rabbits (<1 year) post-bloat episode are highest recurrence risk (60-80% within 3-6 months if diet not dramatically improved); implement most aggressive preventive diet management. Older rabbits (>4 years) with history of bloat require lifelong strict dietary management. (10) OWNER EDUCATION: provide written diet/housing guidelines; most bloat recurrences preventable with proper management. Include warning signs list (abdominal pain, appetite loss, reduced fecal output) for early intervention if recurrence threatened.",
        "prevention_ja": "重大予防—再発リスク高（70-80%）：(1) 食事管理—高繊維食強制：最小18-20%粗繊維（最適は20-30%）；チモシー干し草ベース食がゴールドスタンダード（チモシー干し草70-80%、商業ペレット15-20%、小量野菜5%）。ペレット粗繊維含量確認。質の良いペレット：チモシーベース > アルファルファベース。保管冷涼/乾燥；ペレット>6ヶ月古いの回避。給餌しないの：「トリート」ペレット（着色ボール、ヨーグルト粒—過剰砂糖）、大量果物、高シュウ酸野菜、カビ/湿潤干し草。(2) 急激な給餌変化回避：食事切替必要ならゆっくり7-10日（10-15%新食/日、cecal菌叢適応許可）。(3) ストレス低減—ストレスが主一次トリガー：最小化過度ハンドリング、大音声、過密飼育、社会的葛藤。環境エンリッチメント。(4) 歯疾患予防—定期歯科検査；不正咬合または歯疾患→疼痛→胃無気力→鼓脹リスク。(5) 水分維持：新鮮水ad lib。(6) 体重管理：肥満がリスク増加。(7) 薬物予防：オピオイド回避。抗菌薬回避。(8) 運動と運動：毎日2-4時間自由範囲ホップ/運動。(9) 年齢/リスク階層化：若いウサギ（<1年）再発リスク最高（60-80% 3-6ヶ月以内）；最も積極的な予防食管理実装。高齢ウサギ（>4年）は生涯厳格な食事管理。(10) 飼い主教育：書き込み食事/住宅ガイドライン；再発予防可能。",
        "prognosis": "EXCELLENT (>95% survival) if treated within FIRST 1-2 HOURS of symptom onset. Recovery rapid: pain relief within 30-60 min decompression, appetite recovery typically within 4-6 hours, normal fecal output within 6-12 hours. GI motility normalization expected 24-48 hours post-treatment. Complications rare if early intervention. GOOD (70-85% survival) if treated within 2-4 HOURS. Gastric wall ischemia developing but potentially reversible if perfusion restored. Some animals experience transient appetite suppression or loose stools for 3-5 days post-treatment (mild secondary dysbiosis from antibiotic therapy, self-limiting with probiotic supplementation). GUARDED (40-60% survival) if treated 4-6+ HOURS after symptom onset. Significant gastric wall ischemia likely with possible areas of mucosal necrosis. Peritonitis risk increased if rupture occurred. Post-operative recovery 1-2 weeks with feeding complications possible (slow appetite return, temporary dysbiosis). Recurrent bloat episodes within 1-3 months common if diet management inadequate post-discharge. POOR (<20% survival) if presentation DELAYED >6 HOURS or GASTRIC RUPTURE confirmed. Peritonitis, septic shock, cardiovascular collapse likely. Even aggressive surgical intervention carries 40-50% mortality in rupture cases; if peritonitis widespread, euthanasia often recommended due to poor quality of life post-recovery. SPECIAL CONSIDERATIONS: (1) PRE-OPERATIVE SHOCK SEVERITY—rabbits with MAP <60 mmHg at presentation, severe metabolic acidosis (pH <7.20, base deficit >-10 mEq/L), or elevated lactate (>4 mmol/L) have significantly poorer prognosis (30-40% mortality even with treatment). (2) RECURRENCE RISK—approximately 50-70% of rabbits that recover from first bloat episode experience recurrence within 3-6 months if diet/management not substantially improved; recurrence episodes have better prognosis (treatment familiar to owner, usually caught earlier) but multiple episodes dramatically reduce quality of life. (3) CHRONIC SEQUELAE—approximately 10-20% of survivors develop recurrent ileus or chronic mild GI dysmotility requiring long-term dietary management (high-fiber diet mandatory indefinitely). Some rabbits exhibit behavioral anxiety surrounding eating post-bloat (conditioned response to pain memory); behavior modification (frequent positive feeding experiences, environmental management) helps.",
        "prognosis_ja": "優秀（>95%生存）—症状発症後最初の1-2時間内で治療。回復迅速：疼痛緩和30-60分除圧後、食欲回復典型的に4-6時間、正常便産生6-12時間。GI運動正常化予想24-48時間。合併症稀。良好（70-85%生存）—2-4時間内で治療。胃壁虚血発展しているが可能性がある逆転灌流回復。一部動物が経験一時食欲抑制または緩い便3-5日間。慎重（40-60%生存）—4-6+時間後治療。有意な胃壁虚血。腹膜炎リスク増加。術後回復1-2週間。不良（<20%生存）—提示遅延>6時間または胃破裂確認。腹膜炎、敗血症ショック、心血管虚脱。専門考慮：(1) 術前ショック重症度—MAP <60 mmHg、重症代謝性アシドーシス、乳酸上昇が有意に悪い予後。(2) 再発リスク—約50-70%。(3) 慢性後遺症—約10-20%。",
        "onset_pattern": {"acute"},
        "age_predisposition": {"adult", "young", "senior"},
    },
